{
"David Hume looked inside for a self and found only thoughts and feelings passing by. The Buddha found the same, and then asked what follows if we stop treating them as mine.": "82dc02ca6b32",
"Descartes doubted everything he could and found one thing left: I am, I exist. From this he concluded that he was a thinking substance, a single thing that persists and has thoughts as its states. The first step is one a Buddhist can accept, since no one denies that thinking is going on. The second step is the one that the not-self teaching refuses. Descartes moved from there is thinking to there is a thing that thinks, and that thing is the ātman under a French name.": "702602dca5a7",
"Giving up the idea of a fixed self does not make you care less. People who see it clearly tend to care more, because the wall between me and you has become thinner.": "ec9b456d4b9a",
"Hume repeated Descartes experiment and reported a different result. When I enter most intimately into what I call myself, I always stumble on some particular perception or other, of heat or cold, light or shade, love or hatred, pain or pleasure. I never can catch myself at any time without a perception. He concluded that a person is nothing but a bundle or collection of different perceptions, and that the identity we ascribe to the mind is only a fictitious one. This is the second premise of the Buddhist argument, reached independently and stated with great force. Humes account stops there. He has no theory of how the bundle is tied together, and in an appendix he admits it: I find myself involved in such a labyrinth, that, I must confess, I neither know how to correct my former opinions, nor how to render them consistent.": "9c26b6a936b7",
"Later teachers made the positive account explicit. What we call a person is a designation applied to the aggregates when they occur together, as the word chariot is applied to assembled parts (Saṃyutta Nikāya 5.10). The aggregates arise and cease in dependence on conditions, and one moment conditions the next. That causal stream is enough for memory, for moral responsibility, and, in the traditional teaching, for rebirth. No owner travels along it.": "5f21144112ea",
"Parfit defends what he calls reductionism. A persons existence consists in the existence of a brain and body and the occurrence of a series of connected physical and mental events. There is no further fact, no separately existing subject. He then draws consequences that Hume did not. What matters for survival is the continuation of the mental stream, whether or not we call the result me. The boundary between my future and other peoples is less deep than it seems, and so concern for others becomes more rational and the fear of death less so. He notes that the Buddha would have agreed, and he prints Buddhist texts in an appendix to make the point.": "1f1f90e1d659",
"Parfit is the closest. He holds all three parts of the Buddhist position: that there is no separate self, that a person is a causally connected series, and that seeing this changes how one should live. Hume holds the first with unsurpassed clarity, has no account of the second, and treats the third as a joke at his own expense, going off to play backgammon. Descartes holds none of them, and his cogito is the best Western statement of the view the Buddha was arguing against.": "2812e7d65c83",
"The Buddha did not say that you do not exist. He said that nothing you can point to is a fixed, lasting owner of your experience.": "3b251b76f320",
"The Buddhist tradition draws a distinction that neither Hume nor Parfit has. It separates the belief in a self from the sense of self. The belief, called identity view (sakkāya-diṭṭhi), is a matter of opinion and can be given up by understanding an argument. The sense, the conceit I am (asmimāna), is something else. In one discourse the monk Khemaka, who is ill, is questioned by his fellows. He says that he does not regard any of the five aggregates as this I am, and yet the feeling I am has not left him. He compares it to the scent that clings to a cloth after it has been washed clean (Saṃyutta Nikāya 22.89). This is exactly Parfits situation: convinced by the arguments, and still unable to feel what he believes.": "480e04d322d7",
"The likeness to Parfit ends at four places. First, he reaches the view by argument about imaginary cases, and says frankly that he cannot always believe it: the conviction fades when he stops thinking about the arguments. Second, the continuity he describes is psychological, and it ends at death. The Buddhist stream includes the body, runs on causal conditions of which memory is only one, and is traditionally held to continue from life to life. Third, Parfit is content to say that persons reduce to physical and mental events and to stop there. The Madhyamaka philosophers pressed on and argued that those events have no independent nature either. Fourth, and most important, Parfit offers a conclusion and the Buddha offers a training.": "c6d039888731",
"The path is the answer to that gap. Ethical conduct loosens the habits that keep the sense of self inflamed. Concentration steadies attention enough to watch the aggregates arise and pass. Insight is the repeated, direct observation that each of them is impermanent and ownerless, until the perception is no longer an inference. The tradition says that the belief falls away early and the conceit only at the end. Western philosophy has the argument, and in Parfit it even has the report of what believing it is like. It has nothing corresponding to the method.": "2479c98cccec",
"The teaching is aimed at suffering. Most of our distress comes from defending a self that turns out, on inspection, to be a process.": "626f5143231a",
"The teaching was first given, according to the tradition, in the Buddhas second discourse, to the five companions who had practiced austerities with him. He takes the five aggregates (khandha) into which a person can be analyzed, namely bodily form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness, and says of each that it is anattā, not-self. He gives two reasons. The first is about control. Form is not-self. For if form were self, it would not lead to affliction, and one could have it of form: Let my form be thus, let my form not be thus. We cannot command the body not to age or a feeling not to arise. The second reason is put as a series of questions. Is form permanent or impermanent? Impermanent. Is what is impermanent satisfying or unsatisfying? Unsatisfying. Is what is impermanent, unsatisfying, and subject to change fit to be regarded thus: This is mine, this I am, this is my self? The monks answer that it is not (Saṃyutta Nikāya 22.59).": "1a658afb632f",
"Think of a river. It is real and it has a name, and there is no water in it that stays.": "b2d26148272f",
"This is something to observe more than something to believe. Watch a feeling arise and fade, and ask who owns it.": "60fa181ad384",
"Three habits of English cause most of the misunderstanding. The first is the phrase no self, which listeners hear as you do not exist. Not-self is closer to the Pāli and to the method: of each thing that you take to be you, see that it is not. The second is the word soul. Many in a Western audience will assume that what is being denied is the Christian soul, and will take the teaching as an attack on religion. It helps to say that the target is any fixed owner or controller of experience, religious or secular. The third is the fear of nihilism. It should be said early and plainly that the person who makes promises, feels pain, and deserves kindness is not being denied.": "69e77b364ebf",
"Three things about the passage are easy to miss. The Buddha says of each aggregate that it is not self. He does not say here, in so many words, there is no self. On another occasion a wanderer named Vacchagotta asks him directly whether there is a self, and then whether there is no self, and both times the Buddha stays silent. He explains afterward that to say there is a self would side with the eternalists, and to say there is no self would side with the annihilationists and leave the questioner more confused than before (Saṃyutta Nikāya 44.10). Second, the target is a particular idea of self that was current in his day: something permanent, blissful, and in control. Third, the teaching is offered as something to be seen and not merely assented to. The discourse ends with the five listeners liberated by what they have understood.": "9f5b69481690"
}
